<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Rome</h1><p class="st-meta">1144 CE – 1193 CE (approx.) · capital Rome</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A medieval Roman republic that revived the Senate on the Capitol in 1144 and briefly challenged the popes' rule over their own city.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1143–1144 the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-ii">Pope Innocent II</a> and his aristocratic allies, revived the Senate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> and dated their documents from the 'renewal of the Senate'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giordano-pierleoni">Giordano Pierleoni</a> was named patrician.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The commune was governed by an elected Senate of up to 56 members, drawing on memories of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. From 1145 the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-of-brescia">Arnold of Brescia</a> preached against papal temporal power and supported the republic. It struggled against the popes and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>, and was opposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, who handed Arnold over for execution in 1155. Rome also fought neighbouring towns such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibur">Tivoli</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1188 a pact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iii">Pope Clement III</a> recognised the Senate in return for papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Around 1193 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedetto-carushomo">Benedetto Carushomo</a> replaced the many senators with a single senator, and the commune's independent phase ended, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> continued as a city office under the popes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/commune-of-rome.jpg" alt="Commune of Rome" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Ddurbmonnejg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traditional_flag_of_Rome.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1144 CE – 1193 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rome</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Giordano Pierleoni, Benedetto Carushomo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romanesco, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Roman Senate (1144), Commune of Rome (1144)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1168&amp;focus=commune-of-rome" aria-label="Commune of Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4984 686 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Rome lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5304 942L5309 945M5304 942L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5309 945h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rome. <a href="/atlas?year=1168&amp;focus=commune-of-rome">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
